# Playwright scratch trees. `just test-e2e` creates
# `src/tests/gui/non-nix-build/tmp/ct-<fixture>-<random>/` per run; they are
# per-run temporaries with randomized suffixes and must never be committed.
/src/tests/gui/non-nix-build/

# Run lock for the PLAT-7 boundary mutation harness. Held with flock for the
# lifetime of a run so a second instance in the same worktree refuses to start;
# two instances corrupt each other's in-memory restores.
src/frontend/viewmodel/tests/unit/.plat7-boundary-mutation.lock

# The same, for PLAT-8's I/O and capability-sandbox harness. It is a SECOND
# lock rather than a shared one: the two harnesses have different subject sets
# and different recorded control digests, and one lock would serialise runs
# that do not need to be serialised while telling a reader they collide.
# (They DO both touch ci/test/plugin-reactive-boundary.sh, so they must not run
# at the same time — that is a fact about the tree, stated in each harness's
# header, not something either lock enforces for the other.)
src/frontend/viewmodel/tests/unit/.plat8-io-mutation.lock

# The same again, for PLAT-9's surfaces, degradation and fault-boundary
# harness. A THIRD lock for the same reason the second exists: a different
# subject set and different recorded control digests. Like the other two it
# does not serialise against its siblings, and like them the reason is stated
# rather than enforced — PLAT-9's harness mutates `manifest.nim` and `host.nim`,
# which PLAT-8's also quotes, so the three must not run at the same time.
src/frontend/viewmodel/tests/unit/.plat9-surface-mutation.lock

# PLAT-10's mutation run lock — the exclusive `flock` taken by
# `run-plat10-distribution-mutations.py` before it touches anything, and held
# for the whole run. A FOURTH lock for the reason the second and third exist: a
# different subject set and different recorded control digests. Like the other
# three it does not serialise against its siblings, and like them the reason is
# stated rather than enforced — PLAT-10's harness mutates `host.nim`, which
# PLAT-8's and PLAT-9's also quote, so the four must not run at the same time.
src/frontend/viewmodel/tests/unit/.plat10-distribution-mutation.lock

# PLAT-11's mutation run lock — the exclusive `flock` taken by
# `run-plat11-definitions-mutations.py`, held for the whole run. A FIFTH lock,
# and the first whose subject set is DISJOINT from its siblings': PLAT-11
# touches `common/project_definitions/`, `ct/launch/project_definitions_dir.nim`
# and `viewmodel/viewmodels/point_collection_source.nim`, and none of the other
# four quotes any of them. So this one genuinely may run beside the plugin
# harnesses — which is worth writing down precisely because the other four's
# notes say the opposite about each other, and a reader who skims would assume
# the rule is "never two at once".
src/frontend/viewmodel/tests/unit/.plat11-definitions-mutation.lock

# PLAT-12's mutation run lock — the exclusive `flock` taken by
# `run-plat12-visualiser-mutations.py`, held for the whole run. A SIXTH lock,
# and the note above it needs a correction rather than a repetition: PLAT-11's
# entry says its subject set is disjoint from its siblings' and that it "may
# run beside the plugin harnesses". The MUTATION SUBJECTS are disjoint and that
# sentence is true of them; what it does not cover — and what
# Verification-Harness-Traps records as residue 12 — is that a harness's SUITES
# compile files another harness MUTATES. PLAT-11's suites compile PLAT-8's
# subject, and a PLAT-8 arm reddens PLAT-11's unmutated suite. The locks do not
# serialise against each other, so the safe rule is the simple one: never run
# two harnesses at once, whatever their subject sets say.
#
# PLAT-12's own subjects are `common/value_presentation/`,
# `common/value_visualisers.nim`, `viewmodel/store/value_media_degradation.nim`
# and the terminal's two variables-pane modules. Its suites compile
# `common/project_definitions/` — PLAT-11's subject — which is the same hazard
# in the other direction.
src/frontend/viewmodel/tests/unit/.plat12-visualiser-mutation.lock

# PLAT-13's mutation run lock — the exclusive `flock` taken by
# `run-plat13-executable-mutations.py`, held for the whole run. A SEVENTH lock,
# and the rule PLAT-12's note settled on applies to it unchanged: NEVER TWO
# HARNESSES AT ONCE, whatever their subject sets say.
#
# PLAT-13's own subjects are `common/project_trust.nim`,
# `common/project_wasm.nim`, `common/project_executables.nim`,
# `ct/launch/project_trust_store.nim` and
# `ct/launch/project_executable_tier.nim` — disjoint from every sibling's. Its
# SUITES are not: they compile `plugin_model/capabilities.nim` (PLAT-8's
# subject), `common/project_definitions/` (PLAT-11's) and
# `ct/launch/grant_store.nim` (PLAT-10's), so an arm in any of those three
# harnesses reddens this one's unmutated control and it would abort at
# `CONTROL IS NOT GREEN` — or score a kill it did not earn.
src/frontend/viewmodel/tests/unit/.plat13-executable-mutation.lock

# The terminal image-rendering harness's run lock
# (`src/common/terminal_graphics/run-plat14-image-mutations.py`), ignored for
# the same reason as the six above: it records the holding process's pid, host
# and working directory, so it is machine-local and would churn on every run.
#
# This harness's subjects are the `terminal_graphics/` package plus the TUI's
# image-capability decision and its CLI flag. Its SUITES are not disjoint from
# its siblings': `test_terminal_media_capability.nim` compiles
# `value_presentation/surfaces.nim` and `value_visualisers.nim`, which belong to
# the declarative-visualiser harness, so an arm there reddens this one's
# unmutated control. Run one harness at a time.
src/common/terminal_graphics/.plat14-image-mutation.lock

# PLAT-15's mutation run lock — the exclusive `flock` taken by
# `run-plat15-visual-mutations.py`, held for the whole run. A NINTH lock, for
# the reason every one before it exists: a different subject set and a
# different recorded control-digest file, so one lock shared with PLAT-14
# would serialise two runs that have nothing to say to each other while still
# leaving each free to corrupt the other's restores.
#
# (The two harnesses share TWO subject files — `cell_render.nim`, which
# PLAT-15 exported `sourceRectOfSubCell` from, and `image_capability.nim`,
# which PLAT-15's pane suite compiles — so they must not run at the same time.
# That is a rule in both headers, not something either lock enforces for the
# other.)
#
# IT IS LISTED HERE BECAUSE PLAT-14's WAS NOT, AND `git add -A` STAGED IT. A
# run lock is a runtime artefact of a developer's own worktree; committing one
# publishes a pid and a hostname and makes every subsequent run show a dirty
# tree.
src/common/terminal_graphics/.plat15-visual-mutation.lock

# PLAT-16's mutation run lock — the exclusive `flock` taken by
# `src/frontend/tui/app/tests/run-plat16-mutations.py`, held for the whole run
# and acquired BEFORE the baseline digests are read.
#
# A TENTH LOCK, for the reason every one before it exists: a different subject
# set, so one lock shared with PLAT-15 would serialise two runs that have
# nothing to say to each other while still leaving each free to corrupt the
# other's restores.
#
# THIS HARNESS'S SUBJECTS OVERLAP THE TUI'S OTHER HARNESSES, and the rule is
# the same one PLAT-14 and PLAT-15 state about each other: `app/runtime.nim`,
# `app/views/shell.nim` and `app/input/keymap.nim` are also PLAT-6's subjects,
# so an arm here reddens `run-plat6-mutations.py`'s unmutated control and vice
# versa. RUN ONE HARNESS AT A TIME.
#
# It is at the repository root rather than beside the script because the
# subjects span `src/frontend/tui/` and `src/frontend/viewmodel/`, and a lock
# inside one of them would name only half of what it protects.
.plat16-mutations.lock

# PLAT-17's mutation run lock — the exclusive `flock` taken by
# `src/frontend/viewmodel/tests/unit/run-plat17-wasm-mutations.py`, held for
# the whole run and acquired BEFORE the baseline digests are read.
#
# AN ELEVENTH LOCK, and the first whose protected set leaves this repository:
# arms W1-W4 mutate `../nim-everywhere/src/nim_everywhere/async_compat.nim`,
# the sibling checkout `config.nims` puts on the Nim path. A second run
# starting between this one's digest read and its first mutation would record
# a mutated sibling-repo file as the control bytes, in a repo whose own git
# hooks know nothing about this harness.
#
# The rule PLAT-12's note settled on applies unchanged and matters more here:
# NEVER TWO HARNESSES AT ONCE, whatever their subject sets say. PLAT-17's own
# subjects (`ci/lib/`, `justfile`, `src/common/paths.nim`, the two manual
# probes, `async_compat.nim`) are disjoint from every sibling harness's, but
# its GRADERS are not: `ci/test/vm-unit-wasm-parity.sh` compiles the whole
# `vm-unit` file set on two backends, which includes every suite every other
